// =============================================================================
// SERVERLESS → ON-PREMISES ON AWS WITH NO INBOUND FIREWALL PORT: REVERSE SSH TUNNEL
//
// JSONC for you to read; emit PLAIN JSON (strip the // lines) when you write
// architecture.md. Read reference/style-networking.md first. Prefer the Private Network
// Gateway (serverless-png-aws.jsonc) when it is an option; this is a fallback.
//
// HOW IT WORKS ON DATABRICKS (use this to adapt the diagram to the user's network):
//   • Serverless compute reaches the customer's VPC the same way as the PrivateLink
//     pattern (serverless-privatelink-nlb-aws.jsonc): a private endpoint rule in the
//     workspace's NCC → AWS PrivateLink → a VPC endpoint service → a Network Load
//     Balancer. Here the NLB's targets are proxy EC2 instances.
//   • On-premises tunnel hosts run autossh and open OUTBOUND SSH connections to the
//     proxy EC2s, over the existing network path (Transit Gateway + Direct Connect /
//     Site-to-Site VPN). The corporate firewall never opens an inbound port.
//   • The proxy EC2s run socat: they accept the tunnels and expose each on-premises
//     resource's port on their network interface. Serverless traffic arrives at the NLB,
//     goes to a proxy, through the tunnel, and the tunnel host forwards it to the
//     resource.
//   • When to use: PNG is not an option, the resources are reachable from the cloud over
//     an existing path (Direct Connect, S2S VPN, SD-WAN), but the firewall blocks inbound
//     traffic from the cloud.
//   • Watch out: not managed; the customer provisions, monitors and maintains the proxies
//     and the tunnel hosts. At least two proxies behind the load balancer so one tunnel
//     failure does not cut connectivity, and a distinct port mapping on each proxy for
//     each on-premises resource.
//
// WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES (layout):
//   • The hops run in traffic order: serverless plane → shared services VPC (NLB, then proxies) →
//     Transit Gateway → the link → the tunnel host → the resources. `track` values line each hop
//     up with the next across the containers, so the path reads straight.
//   • Two colours, two meanings: red = serverless traffic in the cloud and the last hop
//     on-prem; green = the SSH tunnel, which carries that traffic between proxy and tunnel
//     host. The tunnel's direction (opened from on-prem) is in the tunnel host's `desc` and
//     the edge label, so every edge can still read left → right.
//   • Two proxies / two tunnels are ONE tile ("Proxy EC2 ×2") and one tunnel line: the
//     redundancy is a fact to state, not a reason to double the drawing.
//   • The PrivateLink + endpoint service hop is folded into the NLB tile's `desc`; the
//     dedicated reference draws it out when that is the point.
//
// ADAPT IT: name the user's resources and ports. With Site-to-Site VPN only, or SD-WAN,
// rename the link; there is no Direct Connect gateway on this slide's path to add. Azure:
// the same idea with a Standard Load Balancer + Private Link service and proxy VMs.
// =============================================================================
[
